msgid "Sorting"
msgstr "ソート"

msgid ""
"Panache passes `io.quarkus.panache.common.Sort` directly to `find`, `list`, and `stream` methods.\n"
"With Quarkus Data, sort the `BlockingDataQuery` returned by `find()` or `findAll()` using Jakarta Data `Sort` or `Order`."
msgstr ""

msgid "For multiple sort criteria, use `jakarta.data.Order`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Jakarta Data `Sort` supports ascending, descending, and ignore-case variants, but it does not expose Panache `Sort` features such as null precedence or `disableEscaping()`.\n"
"Use an explicit HQL `order by` expression when that Hibernate-specific behavior is required."
msgstr ""

msgid "Pagination and ranges"
msgstr ""

msgid "Paging and ranges are available from nested query operations:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`query.page(pageIndex, pageSize)`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`query.pages().page(pageIndex, pageSize)`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`nextPage()`, `previousPage()`, `firstPage()`, `lastPage()`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`query.pages().next()`, `previous()`, `first()`, `last()`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`hasNextPage()`, `hasPreviousPage()`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`query.pages().hasNext()`, `hasPrevious()`, returning `Boolean` instead of `boolean`."
msgstr ""

msgid "`pageCount()`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`query.pages().count()`, returning `Long` instead of `int`. Update variables and custom method return types that receive the page count to `long` or `Long`."
msgstr ""

msgid "`page()`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`query.pages().request()`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`range(startIndex, lastIndex)`"
msgstr ""

msgid "`query.limits().range(startIndex, lastIndex)`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`pages().page(pageIndex, pageSize)` is the closest mechanical replacement and retains Panache's zero-based page index.\n"
"The page request type is `jakarta.data.page.PageRequest` rather than `io.quarkus.panache.common.Page`.\n"
"Its `page()` value is one-based: replace reads of `query.page().index` with `query.pages().request().page() - 1`.\n"
"As with Panache, do not combine fixed ranges and paging on the same query."
msgstr ""

msgid "Adopt generated repository methods where useful"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can keep existing query strings when moving calls from a Panache entity or repository to `ManagedRepository`.\n"
"The repository's `find`, `list`, `count`, `update`, and `delete` methods interpret these strings at runtime, just as Panache does.\n"
"For example, with an injected `PersonRepository people`:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Panache Query Language shorthand: `\"status\"` expands to a query that matches the `status` field to the supplied value."
msgstr ""

msgid "A full Hibernate Query Language (HQL) query with an indexed parameter: `?1` receives the first supplied value."
msgstr ""

msgid "A named parameter: the `status` entry in the map supplies the value for `:status`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An existing named query declared on the entity with `@NamedQuery`, referenced by prefixing its name with `#`.\n"
"This example assumes `Person.findByStatus` is already declared with a `?1` parameter."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Query options also carry over to the object returned by `find()`.\n"
"Use `withLock()` to request a database lock within a transaction, `withHint()` to pass query hints to Hibernate, and `filter()` to enable an existing Hibernate filter while fetching results.\n"
"Use `project(Dto.class)` to return DTO instances containing selected fields instead of complete entities.\n"
"These methods have the same roles as on `PanacheQuery`; see the comparison in <<query-results>>."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When a custom helper always uses the same query structure, you can instead declare an annotated repository method and let the annotation processor generate its implementation.\n"
"For example, replace this custom method in `PersonRepository`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"with an abstract `@Query` method.\n"
"The following repository also illustrates generated finder and delete methods:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`@Find` builds a query from the method parameters: the `status` parameter matches the entity's `status` field."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`@Delete` matches parameters to fields in the same way, deletes matching entities, and returns the number deleted.\n"
"Call this method within a transaction, as with the corresponding Panache delete operation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`@Query` specifies the query explicitly and binds `:status` to the method parameter.\n"
"This replaces the default method above while retaining its filtering and ordering."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The annotation processor checks the declared queries and parameter mappings when you build the application, so errors such as an unknown entity field can be reported before the method is called.\n"
"The methods have no handwritten bodies; Quarkus Data generates them.\n"
"The call to the custom helper remains the same:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use `@NativeQuery` for SQL when necessary. For runtime-built queries, continue to use repository `find`, `list`, `count`, `update`, and `delete` methods.\n"
"For type-safe dynamic filtering and ordering, consider Jakarta Data restrictions and the generated metamodel; see\n"
"xref:quarkus-data-hibernate.adoc#generated-metamodels[generated metamodels and type-safe queries]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Replace global Panache utilities deliberately"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Panache global static helpers do not have direct Quarkus Data equivalents.\n"
"This includes global `getEntityManager()`, `getSession()`, `getTransactionManager()`, `executeUpdate()`, `setRollbackOnly()`, and `flush()` methods."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For work associated with an entity repository, use `repository.getSession()` and `repository.flush()`.\n"
"For arbitrary HQL, Criteria, or native SQL, inject a Hibernate `Session` or Jakarta Persistence `EntityManager`.\n"
"An injection with only `@Inject` selects the default persistence unit.\n"
"To select a configured named persistence unit, add Quarkus's `@PersistenceUnit` annotation to the injected field.\n"
"For example, to obtain the session for a persistence unit named `users`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Here, `@PersistenceUnit(\"users\")` is a CDI qualifier: it tells Quarkus which persistence unit's session to inject.\n"
"The same annotation can select the persistence unit for an injected `EntityManager`.\n"
"See xref:hibernate-orm.adoc#multiple-persistence-units[Multiple persistence units] for configuration and injection details."
msgstr ""

msgid "For transaction-manager or rollback-only control, use the Jakarta Transactions facilities appropriate to the application."
msgstr ""

msgid "Update tests"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`PanacheMock` mocks static active-record methods and therefore does not apply to Quarkus Data repositories.\n"
"After moving static entity queries to an injected repository, mock or replace that CDI bean using the normal Quarkus test mechanisms, such as `@InjectMock` from `quarkus-junit-mockito`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Existing tests should continue to cover the same persistence behavior.\n"
"Add focused tests for every moved static helper and for intentional query changes, particularly sorting, paging, ranges, and custom identifier handling."
msgstr ""

msgid "Migration checklist"
msgstr "移行チェックリスト"

msgid "Enable the Quarkus Data Hibernate annotation processor and extension."
msgstr ""

msgid "Remove the classic `quarkus-hibernate-orm-panache` extension when converting the application."
msgstr ""

msgid "Migrate blocking Panache entities to `ManagedEntity`, or implement `ManagedEntity.CustomId` for custom identifiers."
msgstr ""

msgid "Replace `PanacheRepository` with `ManagedRepository<Entity>` and `PanacheRepositoryBase<Entity, Id>` with `ManagedRepository.CustomId<Entity, Id>`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Convert repository classes to interfaces, use `default` for custom method implementations, and annotate top-level repository interfaces with `@Repository`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Move active-record static query methods and custom static helpers to repositories or services."
msgstr ""

msgid "Replace Panache `Sort`, `Page`, and `Parameters` usages with Jakarta Data `Sort`/`Order`, `PageRequest`, and `Map` as appropriate."
msgstr ""

msgid "Replace `findByIds` with an annotated finder or a query."
msgstr ""

msgid "Replace uses of global Panache utilities with repository, `Session`, `EntityManager`, or transaction APIs."
msgstr ""

msgid "Update static Panache mocks to CDI repository mocks, and run the relevant tests."
msgstr ""

msgid "Hibernate Reactive with Panache"
msgstr "Hibernate Reactive with Panache"

msgid ""
"This section migrates the Java API from `io.quarkus.hibernate.reactive.panache` to Quarkus Data Hibernate's managed reactive API.\n"
"The examples retain Mutiny `Uni` return types and stateful Hibernate Reactive sessions.\n"
"They use the same `Person` and `Status.ALIVE` model as the section that deals with Hibernate ORM but all entity and repository operations in this part are reactive."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Add `quarkus-data-hibernate` and enable its `quarkus-data-processor` annotation processor by following the\n"
"xref:quarkus-data-hibernate-getting-started.adoc[getting started tutorial].\n"
"Keep `quarkus-hibernate-reactive` and your reactive SQL driver, such as `quarkus-reactive-pg-client` for PostgreSQL.\n"
"Also add `quarkus-hibernate-reactive-panache-common`, which supplies the reactive support used by the generated repositories.\n"
"See the tutorial's xref:quarkus-data-hibernate-getting-started.adoc#reactive[reactive setup] for the dependency configuration."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Remove `quarkus-hibernate-reactive-panache` when converting the application.\n"
"Classic Panache extensions and Quarkus Data Hibernate cannot coexist in the same application.\n"
"Keep `quarkus-hibernate-reactive-panache-common`: this support artifact is distinct from the classic Panache extension you are replacing."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Your entity mappings, reactive datasource configuration, and Hibernate configuration remain applicable.\n"
"Review session and transaction boundaries as described in <<reactive-transactions>>."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As with ORM, replace static entity queries or handwritten CDI repository classes with repository interfaces,\n"
"whose implementation is generated at build time by Quarkus Data.\n"
"For reactive access, use `ManagedEntity.Reactive` and `ManagedRepository.Reactive<Entity>`.\n"
"These use a stateful `Mutiny.Session`, preserving managed-entity dirty checking.\n"
"The blocking `ManagedRepository<Entity>` is a different API.\n"
"For alternative session models, see xref:quarkus-data-hibernate.adoc#session-and-access-models[Session and access models]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Queries return `Uni` results, but the query builder itself is synchronous.\n"
"For example, `people.find(\"status\", Status.ALIVE)` returns a `ReactiveDataQuery<Person>`, and its `list()` method returns a `Uni<List<Person>>`.\n"
"Continue composing and returning the `Uni` so the caller subscribes to the database operation."
msgstr ""

